double pi = CalculatePi(1000000);
Console.WriteLine(pi);
```

In the code above, `terms` is the number of iterations that we use to estimate PI. The higher the value of `terms`, the more accurate the result. However, the time that it takes to calculate the result increases with the number of iterations. Keep in mind that when a certain limit is reached, the result will not be more precise due to the precision and rounding error of the floating-point storage in C#.

This implementation does not use recursion, because recursion would make the operation much slower and would restrict the number of iterations due to the maximum recursion depth.

